'''''Sporobolus maritimus''''', or synonymously as '''''Spartina maritima''''', the '''small cordgrass''',<ref>{{PLANTS|id=SPMA6|taxon=Spartina maritima|accessdate=24 November 2015}}</ref> is a species of [[cordgrass]] native to the coasts of western and southern Europe and western Africa, from the Netherlands west across southern [[England]] to southern Ireland, and south along the Atlantic coast to Morocco and also on the [[Mediterranean Sea]] coasts. There is also a disjunct population on the Atlantic coasts of Namibia and South Africa.

==Description==
''Sporobolus maritimus'' is a coarse, robust, [[Herbaceous plant|herbaceous]] [[perennial plant]] growing gregariously from a creeping rootstock. The plant is {{convert|20|to|70|cm|in|0|abbr=on}} tall, green in spring and summer, and turning light brown in autumn and winter. The [[leaf|leaves]] are slender, {{convert|10|to|40|cm|in|0|abbr=on}} long, and {{convert|0.5|to|1|cm|in|1|abbr=on}} broad at the base, tapering to a blunt point. The inflorescence is a group of two or three unbranched spikes up to {{convert|15|cm|in|0|abbr=on}} long, each with several unstalked, one-flowered, downy spikelets about {{convert|1.25|cm|in|1|abbr=on}} long, which are produced on all sides of the stalk and closely pressed against it. The pointed stem tip does not overtop the highest spikelet. The flowers are greenish, turning yellowish-brown by the winter.<ref>{{cite book |last1=McClintock |first1=David |last2=Fitter |first2=R.S.R. |year=1961 |title=The Pocket Guide to Wild Flowers |publisher=Collins |publication-place=London |pages=286–287 }}</ref>

==Distribution and habitat==
''Sporobolus maritimus'' is native to the west-facing coasts of Europe and North Africa. It occupies a range of habitats including very soft mud and shingle, in minimally exposed areas, away from strong wave action. It occurs on the seaward margins of [[saltmarsh]]es and creeks and may be plentiful in dried up pools in the upper parts of saltmarshes. In the British Isles, it occurs in estuaries in [[Essex]] and in the [[Solent]].<ref name=JNCC>{{cite web |url=https://sac.jncc.gov.uk/habitat/H1320/ |title=''Spartina'' swards (''Spartinion maritimae'')  |publisher=JNCC |accessdate=17 March 2020}}</ref> 

==Hybridisation and decline==
When the related [[Americas|American]] species ''[[Sporobolus alterniflorus]]'' (smooth cordgrass) was introduced to southern England in about 1870, it hybridised with ''S. maritimus'' to give the [[Hybrid (biology)|hybrid]] ''Sporobolus × townsendii''. This then gave rise to a new [[polyploid|allotetraploid]] species ''[[Sporobolus anglicus]]'' (common cordgrass), which is much more vigorous, and has now largely ousted ''S. maritimus'' from much of its native range in Western Europe.<ref name=JNCC/>

